.TH PEPORTS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
peports \- list the exports and imports of EXEs and DLLs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B peports
.RB [ \-ehim ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B peports
prints the export table, the import table, and the machine type of each
PE image, such as an EXE or DLL.
It is like the
.B /exports
and
.B /imports
options of MSVC
.BR dumpbin ,
and narrower and more precise than
.BR "objdump \-p" .
It is pronounced like
.IR purports .
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
The output for each file has up to three parts, in this order, and the
output of several files runs together without file names.
Fields are separated by tabs.
.TP
Machine type
A
.B >machine
line, then a line with a tab, the machine field as
.B 0x
and 4 hex digits, a space, and its name, such as
.BR AMD64 ,
.BR I386 ,
or
.BR ARM64 .
An unrecognized value is named
.BR ??? .
.TP
Exports
An
.B >exports
line, then a line for each named export: a tab, its ordinal, a tab, and
its name.
A forwarded export is followed by a space and its target in angle
brackets.
Exports without names follow, each as a tab, its ordinal, a tab, and
.BR <NONAME> .
This part is omitted when the image has no export table.
.TP
Imports
For each imported DLL, its name at the start of a line, then a line for
each imported symbol: a tab, its hint, a tab, and its name.
A symbol imported by ordinal is a tab, the ordinal, a tab, and
.BR <NONAME> .
This part is omitted when the image has no import table.
.PP
Names are printed as ASCII.
Bytes outside printable ASCII, and the characters
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
and
.BR \e ,
are written as
.BI \ex HH\fR,
so angle brackets in the output always delimit.
An empty name is
.BR <> .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-ei .
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Without
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-i ,
or
.BR \-m ,
.B peports
prints exports and imports.
.TP
.B \-e
Print the export table.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-i
Print the import table.
.TP
.B \-m
Print the machine type.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option, a file could not be read or parsed, or an error writing
output.
.PP
A file that cannot be read or parsed is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
peports: \fIreason\fR: \fIfile\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and the remaining files are still processed.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the DLLs and functions a program imports:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ peports \-i xxd.exe
KERNEL32.dll
	0	CreateFileW
	0	ExitProcess
	...
SHELL32.dll
	0	CommandLineToArgvW
.EE
.RE
.PP
List only the DLLs:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ peports \-i main.exe | grep \-v \(aq^[[:space:]]\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show where a system DLL forwards an export:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd c:/windows/system32
$ peports \-e kernel32.dll | grep \-w DecodePointer
	302	DecodePointer <NTDLL.RtlDecodePointer>
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode the C++ names exported by a DLL built with MSVC, or with GCC:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ peports \-e library.dll | vc++filt
$ peports \-e library.dll | c++filt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B peports
is a debugging tool, not a security tool.
It does not misbehave on malformed input, but for specially crafted
images its reading may differ from the Windows loader's.
.PP
w64devkit links import tables with zero hints unless a DEF file gives
them, so the hint column of programs built with the kit is usually 0.
.PP
Paths may contain any Unicode characters.
Only the first 96 sections of an image are read, and a very large file
is read only up to available memory, which is not an error since the
tables are normally near the start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR c++filt (1),
.BR dlltool (1),
.BR objdump (1),
.BR vc++filt (1)
